# AnyKernel3 flasher zips (kernel zips, some gapps installers) loop-mount
# the APEX payloads to make /apex/com.android.runtime/bin/* exist, because
# CP2A's /system/bin/linker64 is a symlink into that APEX and post-bind
# getprop/toolbox cannot execute without it.
#
# osm0sis's update-binary setcon's itself to su, mounts /apex as a plain
# tmpfs (payload files get the tmpfs label) and losetup's the payloads.
# The loop driver then reads them from worker threads in the kernel
# domain, which on user builds:
#  - cannot use fds owned by the su process, and
#  - cannot read tmpfs-labeled files.
# Both checks return EIO to the mount (measured 24/11, user recovery:
# "avc: denied { read } comm=kworker path=/apex/*.img scontext=u:r:kernel
# tcontext=u:object_r:tmpfs:s0 permissive=0" -> every apex mount fails ->
# flasher aborts with "Unable to determine boot partition").
# system/sepolicy/private/kernel.te whitelists exactly this under
# userdebug_or_eng() (kernel.te:178-179), which is why the same zip works
# on a userdebug recovery: mirror those rules for the recovery variant of
# the user policy. Zero effect on the policy running in the system.
recovery_only(`
allow kernel su:fd use;
allow kernel su:file { read open getattr map };
allow kernel tmpfs:file { read open getattr map };
')
